<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>13</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>

    <!--
      Zero warnings, enforced. A warning that is allowed to survive is a warning
      nobody reads, and 0.3.4 shipped its faults underneath a wall of them.
    -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      Without this the IDE rules in .editorconfig only ever fire inside an
      editor, so a style rule set to error still lets the build through and the
      gate proves nothing.
    -->
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>

    <!--
      IDE0005 (unused using) is the one style rule the compiler refuses to run
      without a documentation file — it fails the build telling you so, rather
      than skipping the rule quietly. CS1591 goes with it: this codebase
      explains itself in prose comments that say why, not in XML doc tags on
      every public member.
    -->
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>

    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Whichever NoMercy.Plugins.Abstractions and NoMercy.Plugins.Mvc that
      scripts/fetch-abstractions last packed into _nupkgs/, which is the media
      server's current release. Floating rather than a number, by the owner's
      decision: this plugin is always built against the newest contract, and a
      server release is never chased by hand here.

      It replaces a half-and-half that could only ever break. The number was
      fixed and the checkout the packages are built from followed master, so
      the two agreed until the server released and then did not: on
      30 August 2026 the server went to 0.1.479, the number still said 0.1.478,
      that version no longer existed to resolve, and NU1603 under
      -warnaserror turned a release commit red.

      What floating costs, said out loud: the same commit built on two days can
      resolve two different contracts, so a build is not reproducible from the
      commit alone. What it was compiled against is in that run's own log,
      which prints the version it packed.
    -->
    <NoMercyContractVersion>*</NoMercyContractVersion>

    <!--
      The version, in the one place every project shares it. PluginIdentity and
      plugin.json carry it too and a test holds all three together: three
      copies of one number is how a server comes to report a version it is not
      running.

      Left to the compiler this was 1.0.0 for a whole sprint's development,
      which matters at exactly one moment — a deploy onto a server that was not
      stopped fails and leaves the old build in place, and that looks exactly
      like a deploy that worked. Somebody checking the file's version to tell
      the two apart would have been reading a number that never changed.
    -->
    <Version>0.3.18</Version>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
